"Every meridian covered so far in this unit has one thing in common: it connects, sooner or later, to an internal organ. Chinese medicine also describes a parallel system of twelve \"sinew channels,\" following broadly similar paths along the limbs but connecting only to muscles, tendons, and joints — never reaching an internal organ at all. Where the main meridians are the system this unit has used to explain referred pain and organ-related symptoms, the sinew channels are the system behind purely physical strain: a pulled muscle, a stiff joint, a movement-based injury with no organ involvement whatsoever.": "53e50d349755",
"Every meridian covered so far in this unit has one thing in common: it connects, sooner or later, to an internal organ. Chinese medicine also describes a parallel system of twelve \"sinew channels,\" following broadly similar paths along the limbs but connecting only to muscles, tendons, and joints — never reaching an internal organ at all. Where the main meridians are the system this unit has used to explain referred pain and organ-related symptoms, the sinew channels are the system behind purely physical strain: a pulled muscle, a stiff joint, a movement-based injury with no organ involvement whatsoever. This distinction matters diagnostically because two complaints that sound similar — pain along the same general path on the leg, for instance — can belong to entirely different systems. Pain following the sinew channel tends to be sharply localized to the affected muscle or joint, worsens with a specific movement, and has an obvious mechanical trigger, like an injury or overuse. Pain following the main meridian is more likely to fluctuate with internal factors — mood, digestion, time of day — the very things this unit's earlier lessons connected to organ function. Recognizing sinew channels as their own system prevents a common mistake: treating an ordinary muscle strain as though it were an organ-related pattern, or the reverse, missing an organ-related pattern by assuming a symptom is purely mechanical. The two systems run alongside each other on the body's surface, but they answer to different causes — which is precisely the kind of distinction this unit has treated as worth making explicit rather than left to guesswork.": "65a3428e44a4",
